NNAAMMEE
       script - make typescript of terminal session

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       ssccrriipptt [options] [_f_i_l_e]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       ssccrriipptt makes a typescript of everything on your terminal session. The
       terminal data are stored in raw form to the log file and information
       about timing to another (optional) structured log file. The timing log
       file is necessary to replay the session later by ssccrriippttrreeppllaayy(1) and to
       store additional information about the session.

       Since version 2.35, ssccrriipptt supports multiple streams and allows the
       logging of input and output to separate files or all the one file. This
       version also supports a new timing file which records additional
       information. The command ssccrriippttrreeppllaayy ----ssuummmmaarryy then provides all the
       information.

       If the argument _f_i_l_e or option ----lloogg--oouutt _f_i_l_e is given, ssccrriipptt saves
       the dialogue in this _f_i_l_e. If no filename is given, the dialogue is
       saved in the file _t_y_p_e_s_c_r_i_p_t.

       Note that logging input using ----lloogg--iinn or ----lloogg--iioo may record
       security-sensitive information as the log file contains all terminal
       session input (e.g., passwords) independently of the terminal echo flag
       setting.

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       Below, the _s_i_z_e argument may be followed by the multiplicative suffixes
       KiB (=1024), MiB (=1024*1024), and so on for GiB, TiB, PiB, EiB, ZiB
       and YiB (the "iB" is optional, e.g., "K" has the same meaning as
       "KiB"), or the suffixes KB (=1000), MB (=1000*1000), and so on for GB,
       TB, PB, EB, ZB and YB.

       --aa, ----aappppeenndd
           Append the output to _f_i_l_e or to _t_y_p_e_s_c_r_i_p_t, retaining the prior
           contents.

       --cc, ----ccoommmmaanndd _c_o_m_m_a_n_d
           Run the _c_o_m_m_a_n_d rather than an interactive shell. This makes it
           easy for a script to capture the output of a program that behaves
           differently when its stdout is not a tty.

       --EE, ----eecchhoo _w_h_e_n
           This option controls the EECCHHOO flag for the slave end of the
           session’s pseudoterminal. The supported modes are _a_l_w_a_y_s, _n_e_v_e_r, or
           _a_u_t_o.

           The default is _a_u_t_o — in this case, EECCHHOO enabled for the
           pseudoterminal slave; if the current standard input is a terminal,
           EECCHHOO is disabled for it to prevent double echo; if the current
           standard input is not a terminal (for example pipe: eecchhoo ddaattee ||
           ssccrriipptt) then keeping EECCHHOO enabled for the pseudoterminal slave
           enables the standard input data to be viewed on screen while being
           recorded to session log simultaneously.

           Note that 'never' mode affects content of the session output log,
           because users input is not repeated on output.

       --ee, ----rreettuurrnn
           Return the exit status of the child process. Uses the same format
           as bash termination on signal termination (i.e., exit status is 128
           + the signal number). The exit status of the child process is
           always stored in the type script file too.

       --ff, ----fflluusshh
           Flush output after each write. This is nice for telecooperation:
           one person does mmkkffiiffoo _f_o_o; ssccrriipptt --ff _f_o_o, and another can
           supervise in real-time what is being done using ccaatt _f_o_o. Note that
           flush has an impact on performance; it’s possible to use SSIIGGUUSSRR11 to
           flush logs on demand.

       ----ffoorrccee
           Allow the default output file _t_y_p_e_s_c_r_i_p_t to be a hard or symbolic
           link. The command will follow a symbolic link.

       --BB, ----lloogg--iioo _f_i_l_e
           Log input and output to the same _f_i_l_e. Note, this option makes
           sense only if ----lloogg--ttiimmiinngg is also specified, otherwise it’s
           impossible to separate output and input streams from the log _f_i_l_e.

       --II, ----lloogg--iinn _f_i_l_e
           Log input to the _f_i_l_e. The log output is disabled if only ----lloogg--iinn
           specified.

           Use this logging functionality carefully as it logs all input,
           including input when terminal has disabled echo flag (for example,
           password inputs).

       --OO, ----lloogg--oouutt _f_i_l_e
           Log output to the _f_i_l_e. The default is to log output to the file
           with name _t_y_p_e_s_c_r_i_p_t if the option ----lloogg--oouutt or ----lloogg--iinn is not
           given. The log output is disabled if only ----lloogg--iinn specified.

       --TT, ----lloogg--ttiimmiinngg _f_i_l_e
           Log timing information to the _f_i_l_e. Two timing file formats are
           supported now. The classic format is used when only one stream
           (input or output) logging is enabled. The multi-stream format is
           used on ----lloogg--iioo or when ----lloogg--iinn and ----lloogg--oouutt are used together.
           See also ----llooggggiinngg--ffoorrmmaatt.

       --mm, ----llooggggiinngg--ffoorrmmaatt _f_o_r_m_a_t
           Force use of _a_d_v_a_n_c_e_d or _c_l_a_s_s_i_c format. The default is the classic
           format to log only output and the advanced format when input as
           well as output logging is requested.

           CCllaassssiicc ffoorrmmaatt
               The log contains two fields, separated by a space. The first
               field indicates how much time elapsed since the previous
               output. The second field indicates how many characters were
               output this time.

           AAddvvaanncceedd ((mmuullttii--ssttrreeaamm)) ffoorrmmaatt
               The first field is an entry type identifier ('I’nput, 'O’utput,
               'H’eader, 'S’ignal). The second field is how much time elapsed
               since the previous entry, and the rest of the entry is
               type-specific data.

       --oo, ----oouuttppuutt--lliimmiitt _s_i_z_e
           Limit the size of the typescript and timing files to _s_i_z_e and stop
           the child process after this size is exceeded. The calculated file
           size does not include the start and done messages that the ssccrriipptt
           command prepends and appends to the child process output. Due to
           buffering, the resulting output file might be larger than the
           specified value.

       --qq, ----qquuiieett
           Be quiet (do not write start and done messages to standard output).

       --tt[_f_i_l_e], ----ttiimmiinngg[=_f_i_l_e]
           Output timing data to standard error, or to _f_i_l_e when given. This
           option is deprecated in favour of ----lloogg--ttiimmiinngg where the _f_i_l_e
           argument is not optional.

       --hh, ----hheellpp
           Display help text and exit.

       --VV, ----vveerrssiioonn
           Print version and exit.

SSIIGGNNAALLSS
       Upon receiving SSIIGGUUSSRR11, ssccrriipptt immediately flushes the output files.

EENNVVIIRROONNMMEENNTT
       The following environment variable is utilized by ssccrriipptt:

       SSHHEELLLL
           If the variable SSHHEELLLL exists, the shell forked by ssccrriipptt will be
           that shell. If SSHHEELLLL is not set, the Bourne shell is assumed. (Most
           shells set this variable automatically).

NNOOTTEESS
       The script ends when the forked shell exits (a _c_o_n_t_r_o_l_-_D for the Bourne
       shell (sshh(1p)), and _e_x_i_t, _l_o_g_o_u_t or _c_o_n_t_r_o_l_-_d (if _i_g_n_o_r_e_e_o_f is not set)
       for the C-shell, ccsshh(1)).

       Certain interactive commands, such as vvii(1), create garbage in the
       typescript file. ssccrriipptt works best with commands that do not manipulate
       the screen, the results are meant to emulate a hardcopy terminal.

       It is not recommended to run ssccrriipptt in non-interactive shells. The
       inner shell of ssccrriipptt is always interactive, and this could lead to
       unexpected results. If you use ssccrriipptt in the shell initialization file,
       you have to avoid entering an infinite loop. You can use for example
       the ..pprrooffiillee file, which is read by login shells only:

           if test -t 0 ; then
               script
               exit
           fi

       You should also avoid use of ssccrriipptt in command pipes, as ssccrriipptt can
       read more input than you would expect.

HHIISSTTOORRYY
       The ssccrriipptt command appeared in 3.0BSD.

BBUUGGSS
       ssccrriipptt places _e_v_e_r_y_t_h_i_n_g in the log file, including linefeeds and
       backspaces. This is not what the naive user expects.

       ssccrriipptt is primarily designed for interactive terminal sessions. When
       stdin is not a terminal (for example: eecchhoo ffoooo || ssccrriipptt), then the
       session can hang, because the interactive shell within the script
       session misses EOF and ssccrriipptt has no clue when to close the session.
       See the NNOOTTEESS section for more information.

